===== managed_config.toml =====
# Observer-managed Codex defaults (managed_config.toml).
# These are DEFAULTS a user may change in-session; Codex reapplies them on
# next launch and they override even CLI --config. Deploy per the README.

# Route Codex's OpenAI traffic through Observer's proxy for accurate token
# capture. This MUST live in the file: Codex 0.130+ drops the launcher's
# `-c openai_base_url` override before the inner app-server makes the call
# (docs/codex-shared-app-server-gotcha.md, V6-2).
openai_base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8820/v1"

# Enable Codex hooks so Observer captures session/permission/user-prompt rows.
[features]
hooks = true

# Pin Observer's MCP server for in-session project-knowledge tools. The npx
# bootstrap self-installs the matching platform binary per node. NOTE: this
# pin delivers in-session TOOLS, NOT telemetry - see the README.
[mcp_servers.observer]
command = "npx.cmd"
args = ["-y", "@superbased/observer", "serve"]

===== requirements.toml =====
# Observer-managed Codex requirements (requirements.toml) - ENFORCED.
# When a user's config/profile/CLI conflicts with a rule here, Codex falls
# back to a compatible value and notifies the user. Deploy per the README
# (system path /etc/codex/requirements.toml, %ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml
# on Windows, or MDM requirements_toml_base64 on macOS).

# Optional: run only managed hooks (ignore user/project/session hooks).
# Do not uncomment by hand: it also switches off Observer's per-node hooks.
# Regenerate with --enforce-managed-hooks, which adds Observer's managed hooks.
# allow_managed_hooks_only = true

# Optional MCP allowlist (Codex [mcp_servers.<name>.identity]). Once this
# table exists Codex disables every MCP server it does not name, and a named
# server runs only when its launch matches the identity: name AND command.
# The identity below is the managed_config.toml pin's exact launch; the string
# form (command = "npx") would not check the arguments. Add a rule for each
# other server your developers may use before uncommenting.
# [mcp_servers.observer.identity]
# command = { executable = "npx.cmd", args = [{ match = "exact", value = "-y" }, { match = "exact", value = "@superbased/observer" }, { match = "exact", value = "serve" }] }
===== README.md =====
# Observer managed-config for OpenAI Codex

Two artifacts, two layers of Codex's policy model:

- **managed_config.toml** - DEFAULTS the user may change in-session (Codex
  reapplies them next launch; they override even CLI `--config`). This is the
  artifact that wires Observer up.
- **requirements.toml** - ENFORCED rules. A conflicting user value falls back
  to a compatible one and the user is notified.

## MCP pin ≠ telemetry

`managed_config.toml`'s `[mcp_servers.observer]` (`npx.cmd -y @superbased/observer serve`) gives
in-session TOOL presence. It does **NOT** deliver telemetry. `observer` is the
same name `observer init` registers on a node, so a node carries one Observer
server. If your `requirements.toml` declares `[mcp_servers]` identity rules,
keep the `[mcp_servers.observer.identity]` rule (commented in the generated file)
or Codex disables the server.

This pin is written for native Windows nodes, where `npx` is an npm `.cmd`
shim: `npx.cmd -y @superbased/observer serve` is the form this client starts
there. Regenerate with `--target-os linux` or `--target-os darwin` for other
nodes.

## Telemetry delivery

Per-turn token capture comes from the **proxy route** (`openai_base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8820/v1"`)
plus `[features].hooks`. The proxy must be running and reachable. (Codex
0.130+ drops the launcher's `-c openai_base_url`; the file value is what works.)

## Deploy

- **Single node (no license):** write `requirements.toml` to
  `/etc/codex/requirements.toml` (Linux and macOS) or
  `%ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml` (Windows; an HKLM
  `SOFTWARE\Policies\OpenAI\Codex` `RequirementsPath` value can relocate it).
  Write `managed_config.toml` to `/etc/codex/managed_config.toml` (Linux and
  macOS) or the user's `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\managed_config.toml` (Windows,
  where the user can edit it). Verify Codex picks them up on launch.
- **Fleet (MDM, macOS):** push `requirements.toml` as the `requirements_toml_base64`
  value - base64 of the file (the CLI prints it with `--print-mdm-base64`).
- **Requirements precedence (highest→lowest):** macOS MDM
  `requirements_toml_base64` → legacy `/etc/codex/managed_config.toml` read as
  requirements (Unix) → cloud-managed requirements → system `requirements.toml`.
  A higher source's value wins; a lower one only fills what is unset.
